# bridgemap.tsv - what each node calls the same network.
#
# Copy this to bridgemap.tsv (same directory) and fill it in. Without it the
# bridge names on the source node are carried across unchanged, which is right
# when both nodes name their networks the same way and wrong the moment they do
# not: vmbr1 on one node and vmbr1 on another are the same string, not the same
# segment, and nothing in either config says which.
#
# Four columns, TAB or spaces, # starts a comment:
#
#   old_node   old_bridge   new_node   new_bridge
#
# * is allowed in any of the first three columns and the first matching row
# wins, so specific rows go above general ones. A row with fewer than four
# columns is refused rather than ignored, because a row that silently never
# matches looks exactly like no map at all.
#
# A bridge with no row here is carried across unchanged and marked UNMAPPED in
# the output. That is deliberate: guessing puts a machine on the wrong segment,
# and the mistake is invisible until something that should answer does not.
#
# This file is read by tools/c2v-prepare.sh when it prints the qm set commands
# for the VM's interfaces. It never changes a running config by itself - the
# operator still types those commands at go-live, by hand, on purpose.

# --- normal use: one row per network that is named differently on the two nodes
# old_node	old_bridge	new_node	new_bridge
#pve6a		vmbr0		pve9a		vmbr0
#pve6a		vmbr1		pve9a		vmbr2

# --- lab use: a * in the bridge column sends every interface to one bridge.
# That is how the VM gets tested on a dead-end OVS bridge while the CT is still
# running - nothing can leave it, so the VM may carry the CT's own addresses.
# The script warns about this row on every run. Take it out before go-live, or
# the production machine comes up on a bridge with nothing on the other end.
#*		*		*		vmbr99
